In article <1990Feb19.234742.1568@chaos.cs.brandeis.edu> 
lis@chaos.cs.brandeis.edu (Elisabeth Riba) writes:
>Most of my questions were answered by a spoiler sheet someone (I don't
>remember who offhand) E-mailed me, but a few other questions:
>
>1) Does it matter which ring goes on which hand?

  No.

>2) How can you fight a floating eye without being paralysed when you
>   don't have a cream pie to smash him with or a blindfold for
>   yourself?

  Blindfolds don't help, for whatever reason.  But you can throw things
at the floating eye, or use wands, without any danger.

>3) Do the different noises coming from sinks & fountains mean
>   anything?

  No.  They only tell you that the sink or fountain exists.

>4) What is this Elbereth thing that everyone talks about?

  If you engrave ('E') Elbereth on the floor, so long as the engraving
remains intact, monsters (most of them, anyway) won't step on it or
attack you while you're standing on it.  You can engrave with your 
hands ('-') or with various other things, but don't try to use your
best weapon.  Um, unless it's a certain special weapon that wizards
start out with . . .  Play around and see what happens.

>5) I read the way to detect what kinds of wands you have without using them up,
>   and to determine ring types by dropping them in the sink--but how do
>   you discover what kinds of potions or scrolls you have without using
>   identify scrolls?

  Sometimes you can find out about potions by throwing them at monsters.
There are no tricks for scrolls -- either identify them or just read them
and see what happens.

>6) Why am I asking all these questions instead of figuring them out on
>   my own?

  Well, (to take seriously a semi-serious question), there are a lot of
things about this game you just can't possibly find out without either
reading the source or reading spoilers, and some of them are the features
that make the game most interesting.

